This software generates G-code for machine tools. Machine tools cut,
crush and throw things. The output has never been validated against
a real machine. Whoever runs it is responsible for checking it
first, and for whatever happens when they do not.


================================================================
THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS
================================================================

The WTFPL above covers the CNCAM source only. The libraries under
vendor/ are other people's work and keep their own licenses, which
you have to honour if you redistribute them:

  three.js            MIT
                      vendor/three/, Copyright (C) 2010-2024
                      three.js authors

  Clipper 6.4.2       Boost Software License 1.0
                      vendor/clipper/, Copyright (C) 2010-2017
                      Angus Johnson

  occt-import-js      LGPL-2.1
                      vendor/occt/, an OpenCASCADE build by
                      Viktor Kovacs. Used for STEP and IGES import.

The LGPL one is the only one with a condition worth reading twice.
CNCAM does not link it statically: occt-import-js.js and its .wasm
are loaded at runtime, by their own worker, over a relative path.
Anyone who receives a copy can swap those two files for a different
build and the app picks the new one up, which is what LGPL-2.1 asks
for. Keep them replaceable and keep this notice with them and you
are fine. Delete vendor/occt/ and you lose STEP and IGES import but
the LGPL question goes away entirely.

See vendor/README.md for versions and upstream sources.
